WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.978
An airway can reopen and still be injured. A moving

00:00:04.978 --> 00:00:08.147
air–liquid boundary passes across epithelial cells as

00:00:08.147 --> 00:00:09.959
the liquid is displaced.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.944
In a laboratory, we could isolate part of that event in

00:00:17.944 --> 00:00:20.395
a parallel-plate flow chamber. The arrangement

00:00:20.395 --> 00:00:23.663
simplifies the lung, but makes the passing interface

00:00:23.663 --> 00:00:24.071
measurable.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:32.116
An interface moves over a cultured monolayer. The cells

00:00:32.116 --> 00:00:35.782
experience the passage of the boundary, not merely the

00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:37.819
presence of air or fluid.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.086
Cell injury was assessed after exposure. The viability

00:00:47.086 --> 00:00:50.268
measurements describe injury in this controlled system,

00:00:50.268 --> 00:00:53.450
not damage in an entire human lung.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.374
We also asked whether changing the cellular response in

00:01:02.374 --> 00:01:05.425
advance would alter susceptibility to the mechanical

00:01:05.425 --> 00:01:05.861
insult.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.628
The study investigated dexamethasone and

00:01:14.628 --> 00:01:17.170
transdehydroandrosterone. In the experiment,

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.620
pretreatment reduced measured injury under the reported

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.255
conditions.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.772
Cell culture is not clinical ventilation. Delivery,

00:01:29.772 --> 00:01:33.568
dose, tissue context and patient outcomes cannot be

00:01:33.568 --> 00:01:35.941
inferred from a chamber alone.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.662
The design is useful precisely because the exposure can

00:01:43.662 --> 00:01:46.938
be specified and compared, experiment after experiment.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.046
A bubble becomes a physical challenge with a biological

00:01:55.046 --> 00:01:58.243
readout. That is where the next question begins.
